WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.679 --> 00:00:07.830
You can now access all of the Sequence Read
Archive's vast public and controlled-access

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:13.460
human datasets in the cloud, transforming
any laptop into a high performance computing

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:19.030
device, and can ease struggles with speed
and storage issues you might encounter using

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:23.460
FTP to download large datasets from the NCBI.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.910
This short video demonstrates the steps needed
to access public SRA data with Amazon Web

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:30.360
Services.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.590
At the end of the video you can link to a
companion video describing access through

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.960
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.390
I'll cover these three activities:
1) Setting up a new cloud instance

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.890
2) Downloading and installing the SRA Toolkit
within your instance

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:51.920
3) Configuring and testing the Toolkit installation.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:58.780
To set up a new cloud instance, start with
Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2).

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:04.610
If you are new to AWS, or just want to try
out accessing SRA data, the Free tier is a

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.469
good option.

00:01:06.469 --> 00:01:11.630
I've logged in to my account, and I'll launch
a new instance.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:20.180
For this demo I'll select the first Linux
server, accept the t2.mirco instance type,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.170
go to Review and Launch.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:29.549
I'll accept the defaults, but be aware that
you may want to edit the Security Group.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:37.490
And make sure that the region is set to us-east-1
in Northern Virginia for efficient, and free,

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:39.460
data retrieval from the SRA.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:47.790
Okay, I'll click Launch, select a key pair
option, and in a few minutes my instance is

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.280
running.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:57.040
I'll connect to my instance using an SSH client,
following the handy instructions right here.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.950
The next steps are to download and install
the SRA Toolkit, and those steps are nicely

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:09.239
laid out in our github wiki at ncbi/sra-tools,

00:02:09.239 --> 00:02:13.950
including installing and configuration.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:19.659
I downloaded and installed the latest version,
2.10.3

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:26.891
And I now want to show you in detail how to
configure the toolkit, because there are a

00:02:26.891 --> 00:02:35.080
few critical steps required to access these
public data in the cloud.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.290
Type vdb-config -i to get an interactive display
for the configuration.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:44.870
The first requirement is to enable remote
access, which should be selected by default.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:50.019
You can navigate this config menu either by
tab or underlined letter.

00:02:50.019 --> 00:02:54.709
Type c, and you can decide whether you want
data cached locally or not, and where you

00:02:54.709 --> 00:02:56.439
want it cached.

00:02:56.439 --> 00:03:02.930
If local cache is enabled, be sure that you
specify a directory with sufficient disk space.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:05.340
The next two settings are critical.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:13.829
Type a to set AWS as the cloud provider, and
type r to set report cloud instance identity.

00:03:13.829 --> 00:03:16.359
That's it for configuring access.

00:03:16.359 --> 00:03:21.730
I'll type s then o to save, and x to exit.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:27.079
If you want to review these steps in more
detail, see section 05 Toolkit Configuration

00:03:27.079 --> 00:03:29.719
on the github wiki page.

00:03:29.719 --> 00:03:33.190
Let's run a quick test of our toolkit installation.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:38.639
Type fasterq-dump and an SRR accession (SRR5368359)

00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:46.229
This run takes several seconds to retrieve,
And there are the two fastq files.

00:03:46.229 --> 00:03:47.239
That's all for this demo.

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:51.290
We’d love to have your feedback and questions.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:04:00.639
For help with SRA data and the toolkit, write
to sra@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:07.919
For feedback and other questions, please write
to suggest@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

